<header><p class="st-kicker">princely state · Mainland Southeast Asia</p><h1 class="st-title">North Hsenwi</h1><p class="st-meta">1888 CE – 1959 CE · capital Hsenwi</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The northern half of the partitioned Shan state of Hsenwi, on the China border, which kept its saopha under British and Burmese rule until 1959.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>In 1888 the British divided the old Shan state of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/hsenwi-state">Hsenwi</a>, torn by civil war, into two parts. North Hsenwi, with its capital at Hsenwi town, covered the north-eastern lands along the frontier with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yunnan">Yunnan</a>, including <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lashio">Lashio</a> and the Kutkai area.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The saopha of North <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/hsenwi-state">Hsenwi</a> governed a large and ethnically mixed territory of Shan, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kachin-people">Kachin</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/palaung">Palaung</a>, Chinese and Wa peoples, with trade routes to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>. The <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/myanmar">Burma</a> Road, opened in 1938 to supply Nationalist China, ran through it via <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lashio">Lashio</a>, and the region saw heavy fighting in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a>. It was part of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/federated-shan-states">Federated Shan States</a> from 1922.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>North <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/hsenwi-state">Hsenwi</a> became part of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shan-state">Shan State</a> in independent <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/myanmar">Burma</a> in 1948, and its saopha surrendered his powers in 1959. The area later became a centre of ethnic insurgency and the border trade around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muse-myanmar">Muse</a>.</p><p class="st-conf">Few sources survive for this state; details are uncertain.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/north-hsenwi.jpg" alt="North Hsenwi" width="320" height="368"><figcaption>Image: User:Jolle · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Shan_States-Map.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1888 CE – 1959 CE</dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>princely state</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Hsenwi</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Theravada Buddhism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Shan</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd>Myanmar</dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Hsenwi (North)</dd></dl></aside></div>
